[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Interoperability in .NET: P/Invoke, COM, and NativeAOT"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern .NET applications often need to communicate with native libraries written in C, C++, or Rust. Understanding how to bridge the managed and unmanaged worlds efficiently is critical for performance-critical systems, legacy integration, and modern deployment models like NativeAOT."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Platform Invocation (P/Invoke)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"P/Invoke allows managed code to call unmanaged functions exported from dynamic link libraries (DLLs) or shared objects. In modern .NET, the LibraryImport source generator is preferred over the legacy DllImport attribute because it moves marshalling overhead to compile-time."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

namespace NativeInterop;

public partial class NativeMethods
{
    [LibraryImport("native-lib.dll", EntryPoint = "AddNumbers")]
    public static partial int AddNumbers(int a, int b);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Component Object Model (COM) Interop"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"COM interoperability enables .NET applications to interact with COM components. Historically reliant on dynamic casting and runtime wrappers, modern .NET features built-in COM source generators to generate optimized COM wrappers at build time, significantly improving startup performance and AOT compatibility."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"using System.Runtime.InteropServices.ComTypes;

// Example of consuming a COM interface using modern source generators
[ComImport]
[Guid("00000000-0000-0000-C000-000000000046")]
[InterfaceType(ComInterfaceType.InterfaceIsIUnknown)]
public interface IExampleComInterface
{
    void DoSomething();
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. NativeAOT Deployment"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeAOT compiles .NET code directly to machine code ahead of time. This eliminates the Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler and reduces startup time and memory footprint. However, NativeAOT imposes strict limitations on dynamic code generation (like Reflection.Emit). All interop signatures must be known at compile time."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use [LibraryImport] instead of [DllImport] for NativeAOT projects to ensure source-generated marshalling."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid passing generic types or complex object graphs across the native boundary without explicit blittable layouts."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Utilize UnmanagedCallersOnly attribute to export managed methods to unmanaged callers efficiently."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public static class NativeExports
{
    [UnmanagedCallersOnly(EntryPoint = "ManagedAdd")]
    public static int ManagedAdd(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging LibraryImport for P/Invoke, modern COM source generators, and preparing your architecture for NativeAOT, you can build high-performance, self-contained .NET applications that interact seamlessly with the operating system and native libraries."}]
